## corpus/conc/atomic_counter.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="400000 100000\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conc.mm.atomic.raw, conc.mm.atomic.raw.3, conc.task.scope
//!
//! kw11 (K5 = A): the concurrency witness. Four scope tasks each add 1
//! to one shared `*u64` 100000 times with `atomic_add` (relaxed: the
//! count needs atomicity, not ordering), then four more each add 1 25000
//! times through a compare-and-swap loop (acq_rel on success, relaxed on
//! failure, the old value retried) on a second word. The counts are
//! exact on every cpu set — `atomic_witness.rs` runs this program under
//! all cores, `taskset -c 0-3` and `taskset -c 0`; the plain-increment
//! twin, `atomic_race_plain.lu`, is a data race and asserts nothing. The
//! checked machine refuses tasks by name (C1 deferred), so on it this is
//! `unsupported`, not a count.
## corpus/conc/atomic_fence.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="42 7\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conc.mm.fence
//!
//! kw11 (K5 = A): `fence(o)` for every order it admits
//! (`[conc.mm.fence]`): `acquire`, `release` and `acq_rel` inside
//! `unsafe`, `seq_cst` also outside it — the one fence the safe tier may
//! spell, because it promises nothing a safe program could misuse. Beside
//! relaxed stores and loads on one task each fence changes nothing a
//! single task observes; what each fence compiles to is
//! `atomic_disasm.rs`'s.
## corpus/conc/atomic_orders.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="load 5 5 5\nstore 1 2 3\nswap 3 4 5 6 7\nadd 8 9 10 11 12\nsub 13 12 11 10 9\nand 8 8 8 8 8\nor 8 9 9 9 9\nxor 9 8 9 8 9\ncas 8 true 9 true 10 true 11 true 12 true 13 true 14 true 15 true 16 true\ncas 17 false 17 false\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conc.mm.atomic.order, conc.mm.atomic.raw.2, conc.mm.atomic.raw.5
//!
//! kw11 (K5 = A): every order each operation admits
//! (`[conc.mm.atomic.raw.2]`) on one `*u64` — a load's three, a
//! store's three, all five for each read-modify-write, and the nine
//! admitted (success, failure) pairs of a compare-and-swap. On one task
//! every order answers what `seq_cst` answers; the checked machine runs
//! each as `seq_cst` (`[conc.mm.atomic.raw.5]`), the native tier
//! compiles each to Cranelift's sequentially consistent atomics, and the
//! release tier passes each to LLVM as written (`atomic_disasm.rs`
//! shows the instructions differ where the hardware has weaker forms).
## corpus/conc/atomic_widths.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="u8 250 4 240 0 48 33 7 true 9 false 9\nu16 65530 4 65520 0 48 33 7 true 9 false 9\nu32 4294967290 4 4294967280 0 48 33 7 true 9 false 9\nu64 4611686018427387904 4611686018427387914 4611686018427387894 6 54 39 7 true 9 false 9\ni8 122 -124 112 0 48 33 7 true 9 false 9\ni16 32762 -32764 32752 0 48 33 7 true 9 false 9\ni32 2147483642 -2147483644 2147483632 0 48 33 7 true 9 false 9\ni64 9223372036854775802 -9223372036854775804 9223372036854775792 0 48 33 7 true 9 false 9\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conc.mm.atomic.raw, conc.mm.atomic.raw.1, conc.mm.atomic.raw.5
//!
//! kw11 (K5 = A, STATUS #31): every atomic operation on every pointee
//! `[conc.mm.atomic.raw.1]` admits, through one zeroed 8-byte allocation,
//! at `Order.seq_cst`. Each line runs store, add, sub, and, or, xor,
//! swap, a compare-and-swap that matches and one that does not, then a
//! load; each read-modify-write yields the OLD value, and the arithmetic
//! wraps at the width (`u8` 250 + 10 is 4; `i8` 122 + 10 is -124 and
//! -124 - 20 is 112). The `u64` line stays below 2^63 (wolf-lang#551).
//! Before kw11 every machine answered fail(E0301) (`fence`, `Order`
//! unresolved); lupin 0.1.46 answers `unsupported` (pinned by version,
//! wolf-interp#194).
## corpus/kernels/lshr_top_bits.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="33554304 1 131071 15 31\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: arith.wrapping
//!
//! s214 (wolf-lang#600, P0): a branch on the top half of a logical
//! shift is taken when it holds. `a` is 0xffffc04000001000 and `b` is
//! 240, each read back out of a List so the mid-end cannot see the
